nodecode-agent-batch is a rewrite of raaaas's raaaas/hermes-agent-batch:

  https://github.com/raaaas/hermes-agent-batch
  commit 064afc5dba8869df703ba055aa2760730c0d30dc
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 raaaas (LICENSE)

The original was a Hermes desktop panel plus two agent tools over one shared
plan: the panel stored the task list, dispatched a phase on a click and read
the repository's runs and pull requests, and the tools read the plan and
wrote the phasing decision. This cell keeps the plan document and its rules
unchanged -- plan.json with tasks, phases, a pending status per phase, and
the same refusals for a task that lands in two phases, none, or one the plan
does not hold -- and reimplements every call in Common Lisp against
NLK:HTTP, with the user's own GitHub token, to the same two endpoints and
with the original's three dispatch inputs (a newline-joined task list, a
context paragraph, a model and a base branch).

What was left out: the desktop panel (a cell has no window; the same five
routes are five tools and the /agent-batch command), the FastAPI backend and
the JS/plugin manifest that served it, and the repository-side agent workflow
-- orchestrator.py, lifecycle/agent.py and agent-batch.yml, which run inside
the user's own repository and install the coding-agent CLIs there. This cell
dispatching is all; what an agent does is that workflow's business. The
agent_batch_reset route became /agent-batch reset rather than a tool, so a
model cannot wipe a plan by accident.
